/**
\page pidt

Peano's particle support {#section:pidt}
===================

's particle handling is a follow-up to the 2015 paper "Two
Particle-in-Grid Realisations on Spacetrees"
(<https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.02435>).

##### A first  pidt run (native usage of particle interface) {#a-first-pidt-run-native-usage-of-particle-interface .unnumbered}

-   Configure  with the flags `–enable-particles`:

    libtoolize; aclocal; autoconf; autoheader; cp src/config.h.in .;
    automake --add-missing ./configure --enable-particles

    These are the two mandatory extensions. You might want to add
    further options.

-   Recompile your  code. This should give you a couple of
    `ToolboxParticles` libraries in the `src/toolbox/particles`
    subdirectory.

-   Ensure that `python` subdirectory is in your Python search path and

-   run the Jupyter notebook in `examples/particles/sph`:

    export PYTHONPATH=../../../python/ jupyter-notebook peano-sph.ipynb

's particle code is lightweight. There are some C++ building blocks, but
the majority of its features are available through the Python API as
`toolbox.particles`. You will need

import peano4.toolbox.particles

I assume that you are familiar from hereon with 's basics, i.e. you know
how to create a project,

Introduce a new particle
------------------------

With the Python API, the generation of a particle is straightforward:

Though conceptionally a plain C++ pretty printer, the DaStGen2 Python
API can create some fancy C++ annotations. You can, for example, specify
ranges in which integers live, or you can prescribe the precision you
wanna have for floating point values. If you have a specialised LLVM
compiler, these annotations will be used to generate aggressively
optimised code.

The  particle has some additional attributes as they are discussed in
Section
[\[section:swift:backbone:data-model\]](#section:swift:backbone:data-model){reference-type="ref"
reference="section:swift:backbone:data-model"} and thereafter. For the
user, it is usually sufficient to recognise that each particle by
default has a position `x` and and a cut-off radius `cutOffRadius`. You
can query and modify both values through `setX`, `getX`,
`getCutOffRadius` and `setCutOffRadius`, respectively.

We next add the particles to the project and we notify the vertices that
there are particles which have to be administered. I discuss why and how
pidt uses vertices in Section
[\[section:pidt:backbone:dual-trees\]](#section:pidt:backbone:dual-trees){reference-type="ref"
reference="section:pidt:backbone:dual-trees"} though users might
(initially) skip such background info.

Once you have added the particle and the particle container `particles`
to your  datamodel, you have to inform all solver steps that they have
to administer the particles.

Let's assume that our project is spanning the namespace
`examples::someparticles`. In the example above where our particle is
called `Particle`, we will end up with two classes:
`examples::someparticles::globaldata::Particle` is the actual particle
class. When you open the generated C++ code, you'll recognise that it is
a 1:1 mapping of the Python attributes onto a C++ class with some
additional routines used by  internally. There is a further class
`examples::someparticles::vertexdata::ParticleSet`. As the name says,
this is the actual data associated with each vertex. It is a
specialisation of `std::vector` over pointers to
`examples::someparticles::globaldata::Particle`.

We summarise that each vertex holds a vector to pointers over particles.
The actual particles reside on the heap. From hereon, you introduce, as
with any  project additional data fields, action sets, and so forth, and
you write you main to actually control the program flow. We assume from
hereon that the reader is familiar with the concept of an program phase
(algorithmic step) mapped onto an action set.

Insert particles into domain
----------------------------

To create a particle, you in principle just allocate it on the heap, and
then you add it to its nearest vertex. Furthermore, there are some
mandatory attributes that aways have to be set. Therefore, I recommend
that you use the toolbox' initialisation routine to set up particles
correctly.

In principle, it does not matter where you create the particle. You can
do so in `touchCellFirstTime()` or in a cell event. I usually use a cell
event and I rely on the helper function `insertParticleIntoCell` to take
care of the vertex association:

\#include \"toolbox/particles/ParticleFactory.h\"

\...::touchCellFirstTime( const peano4::datamanagement::CellMarker&
marker,
peano4::datamanagement::VertexEnumerator\<\...::vertexdata::ParticleSet\>
fineGridVerticesParticleSet, \...
peano4::datamanagement::VertexEnumerator\<\...::vertexdata::ParticleSet\>
coarseGridVerticesParticleSet, \... )

examples::someparticles::globaldata::Particle\* myNewParticle = new
examples::someparticles::globaldata::Particle();

// assume x and cutOffRadius are set to something meaningful
toolbox::particles::init( \*myNewParticle, x, cutOffRadius );

// here's your user code's initialisation myNewParticle-\>setXXX(\...);

toolbox::particles::insertParticleIntoCell( marker, myNewParticle,
fineGridVerticesParticleSet );

There is no need to write such initialisation code yourself. 's particle
toolbox provides a couple of pre-defined particle generators that you
can adopt to your needs. Probably the most popular particle seeds are

-   `InsertParticlesAlongCartesianLayoutIntoUnrefinedCells`

-   `InsertRandomParticlesIntoUnrefinedCells`

Dual trees {#section:pidt:dual-trees}
----------

Particles in our *dual tree storage scheme* are stored within the
spacetree and associated to vertices:

-   A particle resides on a mesh level within the tree such that the cut
    off radius $r \leq h$ if $h$ is the resolution of this level.

-   A particle is associated to the vertex of this level that's closest
    to its centre $x$.

These two conventions effectively span a dual grid over the spacetree if
you think of particles to be stored within cells: Just impose a grid on
top of your actual grid which is shifted by half a mesh lenght.
Particles are contained within these shifted grid's cells.
Alternatively, you can think of the vertices of the tree spanning a
Voronoi diagram, and particles are then held by the cells of this
Voronoi diagram.

Our storage is vertex-oriented whereas most particle literature stores
particles within cells. If we think of a dual multiscale mesh, i.e. a
mesh that is shifed by $h/2$ alon each coordinate axis, then 's approach
also works with cells. Such shifted meshes are often called *dual*
meshes and I therefore speak of a *dual tree*.

If you add the action set
`peano4.toolbox.particles.PlotParticlesInVTKFormat` to your algorithm
step, the resulting file will contain all associativity information. You
can plot to which vertex a particle belongs to.

Here's a hand-written cartoon to explain the particle storage:

![image](61_pidt/dual-tree.pdf){width="25%"}

A tree with three levels. Particles with a large cut-off radius (red)
are held on rather coarse levels. Particles with very small cut-off are
sorted (dropped) into the fine grid levels. Each particle is always
associated to its closest vertex (dotted line). It is the mesh which
holds (owns) the vertices. The toolset's predefined action set can
automatically maintain/update these associations after each grid sweep.
It also moves particles up and down the grid hierarchy if the particles'
cut-off radius changes or the grid changes.

Move particles {#section:pidt:move-particles}
--------------

To move a particle, all you have to do is to alter its position:

myParticle-\>setX( 17.3, 8.2 );

Obviously, any change of a particle position could mean that the
particle is no longer associated to its nearest vertex. We have to sort
them.

 automatically maintains these relations if you add the action set
`peano4.toolbox.particles.UpdateParticleGridAssociation` to your
algorithmic steps. As the convention relies on a smaller-equals sign,
adaptive meshes are automatically supported. If particles travel through
your domain and the mesh is not very fine, they end up in the tree
leaves. If particles have a large cut-off radius, they are stored in
rather coarse levels of the tree.

If you change the cut-off radius or the position of a particle, the
particle will stay within its current cell/vertex within the tree. In
the next grid sweep, it will however we held by the "correct" vertex,
i.e. its grid-vertex association will be updated: The action set
`UpdateParticleGridAssociation` updates the particle-tree association,
i.e. sorts the particles, on-the-fly between two mesh traversals.

While you do not have to worry about how this sorting is realised, it is
important to study the action set's documentation (just open the Python
code) and read when data is consistent. Here is the general invariant
however:

-   The very moment you alter a particle's position, you cannot be sure
    that it is still associated with the nearest vertex. Consequently,
    neighbourhood searchers (cmp. Section
    [1.5](#section:pidt:particle-particle-interaction){reference-type="ref"
    reference="section:pidt:particle-particle-interaction"}) might yield
    invalid results.

-   In the grid sweep after the position change, the particle will be
    associated with the correct vertex (unless you change its position
    once again obviously).

The action set `UpdateParticleGridAssociation` relies on some global
data. Therefore, you have to call `finishTraversal()` on the used
particle set in your main routine on each rank after all rank-local
traversals have termined.

Most codes move a particle in `touchVertexLastTime`. This is convenient
as we know that any particle associated with the respective vertex also
has been touched for the last time. We can safely update it. However, if
we change a particle's position and merge
`UpdateParticleGridAssociation` into the respective algorithmic step,
then the particle might immediately be associated to another vertex
after it has been updated and this vertex might later on become subject
of `touchVertexLastTime`.

Consequently, it can happen that a particle is updated multiple times.
To avoid this, I recommend that you add a boolean to each vertex:

You can then set the value of `MoveState` to `NotMovedYet` in
`touchVertexFirstTime`, set it to `Moved` once we update the position,
but only update its position as long as it has not been moved yet.
Peano's built-in re-assignment of positions does only move particles
around when vertices are touched for the last time, so there's no risk
here that you update a particle in `touchVertexLastTime`, assign it to a
particle which has not been used yet and then reset it wrongly to not
moved again.

It is absolutely key that you never alter the particle positions
throughout the traversal. The only time you can set a new position is
within `touchVertexLastTime`. This 's particle toolbox the time to
re-sort the particles in-between two traversals. If you use
`UpdateParticleGridAssocation`, you typically combine it with
`peano4.toolbox.particles.UpdateParallelState`.
`UpdateParticleGridAssocation` should be the very first action set of a
traversal, and `UpdateParallelState` should follow immediatly after.

If you move around particles, it is important that you merge the two
update action sets into the action set where you change the particle
position (in the last access of a vertex) plus into the subsequent grid
sweep: Once the particle position has changed,  tries to associate the
particle again to the right vertex. If the associativity however has
changed, it might (temporarily) move particles to a coarser level. It is
the subsequent grid sweep that then sorts the particles again into the
right resolution of the mesh plus associates it with the right vertex.
So it needs access to `UpdateParticleGridAssocation`.

A similar argument holds if you parallelise: If a particle leaves a
domain, the owning tree sends it out by the end of the mesh sweep. The
particle is now in transition. It is the subsequent grid sweep which
works it into the target tree and updates its parallel state.

*Tunneling* means that a particle moves more than one mesh size per time
step. In the context of 's multiscale tree structure, this is equivalent
to particles which travel more than their cut-off radius per step.

Tunneling arises if cut-off radii change suddenly, and if meshes are
extremely refined where they should likely not refine.  does support
tunneling in principle, but you can tell the update script if you want
to get a warning if particles tunnel---in some applications, this is
unphysical.

Dual tree traversal and particle-particle interaction {#section:pidt:particle-particle-interaction}
-----------------------------------------------------

In 's *dual tree* storage scheme, we always hold the complete spacetree
with all tree (grid) levels. Particles are always stored on the finest
mesh (tree) level where the mesh size is bigger than the particle's
cut-off radius. Different particles with different cut-off radii might
thus be stored on different tree levels.

 always runs through the mesh top-down, i.e. it traverses the spacetree
in a depth-first fashion. With the definition above, we can realise
particle-particle interactions as follows:

Per cell, the code assembles a `local set`. This is a list of pointers
to those particles that are associated to a cell's $2^d$ vertices. With
the local set, you can make particles held on one resolution level
interact with each other. You get an cubic area of $2h$ side-lenght per
cell: As a particle is always associated to its closest vertex, the
local set holds all particles whose cut-off radius overlap with the
cell.

Adaptivity and particles with different cut-off are not yet captured
through the local set notion. There's thus a second set that I call
active set. The active set is the local set plus all local sets that
have been active on coarser levels within the tree.

The active set is built up top-down while we run through the tree. So it
is a data structure that grows and shrinks while you run through the
meshes. The local set `localParticles` is created on-the-fly every time
you hit a cell or a vertex. Do not alter the active set: You can modify
the values of the particles within an active set, but you should not add
or remove entries from it. The local set can be modified.

If you use the `toolbox.particles.ParticleParticleInteraction` action
set, you pass it some C source code that works with the particles. This
C code has access to three variables: the active set, the local set and
the cell marker (of type `peano4::datatraversal::CellMarker`). Let's
assume that each particle carries some velocity and that we could
cut-off gravity---which we clearly should not.

The straightforward particle-particle interaction then looks
conceptionally as follows:

// Run over local set for (auto& p: localParticles) // Pick only those
particles that reside within the current cell, otherwise // we would
update each particle up to 2\^d times. I do not exploit the // symmetry
of forces here if ( marker.isContained( p-\>getX() ) ) for (auto& pp:
activeParticles) // No interaction with particle itself if (p!=pp)
tarch::la::Vector\<Dimensions,double\> dist = pp-\>getX() - p-\>getX();
\[\...\]

Typical  codes introduce some variables per particle into which they
accumulate data. In `touchVertexFirstTime` we use a vertex for the very
first time. Consequently, we also read the particles associated to this
vertex for the first time and thus set the accumulation variable to the
neutral element 0. Within `touchCellFirstTime`, we now evaluate
particle-particle interactions and write data to the accumulated values.
In `touchVertexLastTime`, we finally have all data accumulated and hence
can update the particle state.

Study the sort algorithms that we use if you update the vertex
(cmp. Section [1.4](#section:pidt:move-particles){reference-type="ref"
reference="section:pidt:move-particles"}). Once you alter a particle
position, particles might not be tied to their closest vertex anymore.
Neighbourhood searches thus might yield invalid results.

Further action sets
-------------------

There are a few further action sets in the toolbox that help you to
write your Lagrangian code:

1.  The action set `toolbox.particles.PlotParticlesInVTKFormat` provides
    a basic particle plotter which dumps particles, their cut-off radius
    plus their particle-vertex associativity. If you have to dump
    further properties, you have to write your own plotter. The
    generated plotter however can serve as a blueprint.

2.  The action set `toolbox.particles.ParticleTreeAnalysis` provides
    some fundamental tree analysis such as a multiscale counting how
    many particles reside within each cell. You'll need some additional
    data structures (per cell) to use this action set.

3.  The action set `toolbox.particles.ParticleAMR` uses the tree
    analysis to guide dynamic grid adaptivity. You can specify how many
    particles are held per cell at least, e.g., while the cut-off radius
    feeds naturally into the AMR.

